# Rational Design of Catalytic Centers in Crystalline Frameworks

## abstract
Crystalline frameworks including primarily metal organic frameworks (MOF) and covalent organic frameworks (COF) have received much attention in the field of heterogeneous catalysts recently. Beyond providing large surface area and spatial confinement, these crystalline frameworks can be designed to either directly act as or influence the catalytic sites at molecular level. This approach offers a unique advantage to gain deeper insights of structure-activity correlations in solid materials, leading to new guiding principles for rational design of advanced solid catalysts for potential important applications related to energy and fine chemical synthesis. In this review, recent key progress achieved in designing MOF- and COF-based molecular solid catalysts and the mechanistic understanding of the catalytic centers and associated reaction pathways are summarized. The state-of-the-art rational design of MOF- and COF-based solid catalysts in this review is grouped into seven different areas: (i) metalated linkers, (ii) metalated moieties anchored on linkers, (iii) organic moieties anchored on linkers, (iv) encapsulated single sites in pores, and (v) metal-mode-based active sites in MOFs. Along with this, some attention is paid to theoretical studies about the reaction mechanisms. Finally, technical challenges and possible solutions in applying these catalysts for practical applications are also presented.

Crystalline frameworks including primarily metal organic frameworks (MOF) and covalent organic frameworks (COF) have received much attention in the field of heterogeneous catalysts recently. Beyond providing large surface area and spatial confinement, these crystalline frameworks can be designed to either directly act as or influence the catalytic sites at molecular level. This approach offers a unique advantage to gain deeper insights of structure–activity correlations in solid materials, leading to new guiding principles for rational design of advanced solid catalysts for potential important applications related to energy and fine chemical synthesis. In this review, recent key progress achieved in designing MOF- and COFbased molecular solid catalysts and the mechanistic understanding of the catalytic centers and associated reaction pathways are summarized. The state-of-the-art rational design of MOF- and COF-based solid catalysts in this review is grouped into seven different areas: (i) metalated linkers, (ii) metalated moieties anchored on linkers, (iii) organic moieties anchored on linkers, (iv) encapsulated single sites in pores, and (v) metal-mode-based active sites in MOFs. Along with this, some attention is paid to theoretical studies about the reaction mechanisms. Finally, technical challenges and possible solutions in applying these catalysts for practical applications are also presented.
## 1. Introduction
Heterogeneous catalysis represents one of the most important technologies already widely applied in the chemical industry because of its unique advantages such as easy separation and recycling, minimization of catalyst pollution in the products, effective handling and process control, and low cost.[1–4] It continues to draw attention from scientific community which aims to develop more efficient and stable catalysts for a wider range of reactions. Heterogeneous catalysis happens at the catalyst surface, which involves the adsorption and activation of reactant molecules on active sites of the solid surfaces, chemical transformation of adsorbed species, and desorption of products. The ultimate goal of heterogeneous catalysis research is to design catalysts with the rational catalytic sites at the molecular level for high efficiency and desired selectivity.[5] It is desirable to synthesize catalysts with small particle sizes and large specific surface areas to increase the utilization of the catalytic materials efficiently. Incorporation of reactive sites on solid supports such as alumina, silica, zeolites, and other mesoporous materials has been a longstanding and well-adopted strategy in the fabrication of economically viable heterogeneous catalysts for many chemical conversion processes.[6] However, the influences of the conventional solid support materials on the active site cannot easily be established due to the complex and ill-defined nature of these materials. Therefore, it is necessary to develop advanced solid catalysts to provide more insightful molecular level understanding in order to significantly improve the efficiency and selectivity of the catalytic site.
Metal organic frameworks (MOFs), as a subclass of coordination polymers, have drawn increasingly significant attention and shown promising potential in the field of heterogeneous catalysis. They usually possess high crystallinity, high surface area, well-defined pore structure, and tunable pore size and local environment.[7–17] Interestingly, the molecular building units consisting of metal ions or clusters coordinated to organic ligands provide the platform for rational design of MOFs with various functionalities as molecular solid catalysts. The well-defined structures offer a great opportunity for understanding the reaction at the molecular level. Different from MOFs, covalent organic frameworks (COFs) are a class of porous polymers in which organic building blocks are linked by covalent bonds. COFs have also been intensively studied in heterogeneous catalysis. Besides the similar properties as those of MOFs, such as high crystallinity, large surface area, and open pore structures,[13,18–20] COFs are stable under acidic and basic conditions due to the strong covalent bonding between organic groups in COFs. Therefore, MOFs and COFs show increasing potentials in the design of mole­ cular solid catalysts for heterogeneous catalytic applications. For example, $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ nodes dispersed in crystalline frameworks of Cu-MOFs are active and regioselective catalysts for 1,3-dipolar cycloaddition reactions with activities and selectivities as high as those of homogeneous catalysts at molecular level.[21] COF-366-Co consisting cobalt porphyrin complexes as catalytically building blocks showed high activity and selectivity for electrocatalytic reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to CO with improved activities to a factor of 26 over the molecular cobalt complex catalyst due to the excellent charge-carrier mobility in the frameworks.[22] Thus, MOFs and COFs provide the unique opportunity to bring together the advantages of the wellcontrolled active sites of transition metal complexes in homogeneous catalysis with recyclability and scalability of solid catalysts in heterogeneous catalysis. In recent years, many excellent review articles have been found in the literature summarizing the strategies in design and development of MOF[7–10,12–17,23–34] and COF[18–20,35] based heterogeneous catalysts and photocatalysts for various types of reactions.[25,26,30,33,34]
In spite of earlier reviews generally introducing the engineering of MOFs and COFs for heterogeneous catalytic applications, this review focuses on rational design of catalytic centers in crystalline frameworks at molecular level. The key accomplishments in the design of MOF- and COF-based molecular catalysts with diverse catalytic sites are summarized based on (i) metalated linkers, (ii) metalated moieties anchored on linkers, (iii) organic moieties anchored on linkers, (iv) encapsulated single sites in pores, and (v) metal-mode-based active sites in MOFs. In addition, the mechanistic understanding of the catalytic centers and associated reaction pathways for the high efficiency and selectivity is highlighted. The technical challenges and prospects for future development of MOF- and COF-based molecular solid catalysts toward practical applications are also presented.
## 2. MOFs and COFs as Platforms for Molecular Solid Heterogeneous Catalysis
## 2.1. The Choice of MOFs and COFs as the Platform
In brief, MOFs and COFs are solids consisting of molecular secondary building units (SBUs) linked together to form porous crystalline frameworks with extended structures (Figure 1). The SBUs are inorganic metal ions or clusters coordinated to bridging organic ligands[36] (Figure 1a), or organic molecules linked via strong covalent bonds[37] (Figure 1b) in MOFs and COFs, respectively. The flexibility in the choice of the SBUs or organic linkers offers the unique opportunity to design various open framework structures with desired chemical functionality in a controlled manner besides their other properties such as high crystallinity, high surface area $(1000{-}10000^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ , welldefined pore structure of tunable sizes $(0.5{-}9.8\ \mathrm{nm})$ , etc.[36] The characteristics of MOFs such as geometry and porosity can be tuned through changing the metal SBUs or organic linkers
