The diverse choice of metal and organic species in MOFs and COFs makes them possible to directly act as molecular catalytic sites in heterogeneous catalysis.[13] More importantly, because of their controllable chemical environment in the micro- and mesopores, MOFs and COFs can serve as solid supports for precise anchoring single atom catalysts or molecular catalysts to achieve targeted reaction pathway and selectivity.[40] In this aspect, the additional role that MOFs and COFs play compared to conventional solid supports lies in the precise control of the local environment of the catalytic site such as electronic density, spatial distribution, metal–metal interaction, etc.[28,41] Furthermore, mild synthesis conditions for MOFs and COFs allow direct incorporation of delicate moieties into the framework as molecular catalytic sites.[42,43] In all these scenarios, understanding of the nature of catalytic sites can be achieved by comprehensive bulk and local materials characterization relying on various imaging and spectroscopic techniques. As a result, the use of MOF- and COF-based molecular solid catalysts can bridge the gap between homogeneous and heterogeneous catalysis via molecular chemistry.[44] The research in this field holds great promises to not only deepening the fundamental understanding but also developing a wide range of high-performance solid catalysts for potential practical applications.
## 2.2. Advantages of MOFs and COFs as Molecular Solid Catalysts
The basic physicochemical properties of MOFs and COFs are summarized in Table 1. These properties can directly or indirectly influence the steps in heterogeneous catalysis, including adsorption and activation of reactant molecules on active sites of the solid surfaces, chemical transformation of adsorbed species, and desorption of products. First of all, the highly crystalline framework, i.e., atomically well defined structure, allows the pinpoint of the location of the catalytic site and mechanistic understanding of the structure–activity correlations.[45] Second, the high surface area of MOFs and COFs means an increase in storage capacity per weight of these materials, which offers the opportunity for incorporation of dense active sites. Third, the well-defined and tunable pore size and structure in MOFs and COFs may not only control the selection of substrate molecules and/or products in catalytic reactions by size,[46] but also benefits to confine functional moieties into pore structures such as single atoms, metal complexes, and organic dyes.[47] The metal nodes, organic linkers, or functional moieties periodically dispersed in the structures can easily be characterized by various techniques toward understanding of reaction mechanism. Lastly, the high thermal and chemical stability of COFs and many MOFs not only allows them amenable to postsynthetic modification, i.e., the incorporation of functional moieties into the crystalline frameworks as catalytic sites, but also keeps them stable for recycling. Recently, some stable MOFs have been developed for catalytic application, such as $\mathrm{Zr}$ -based MOFs (UiO-66, NU-1000, MOF-525, and PCN-225),[48] Cr-based MOFs (MIL-53 and MIL-101),[49,50] and Fe-based MOFs (MOF-74 and MIL-101).[51] On the other hand, COFs are more stable in adverse conditions due to the existence of strong covalent bonds. For example, PCN-225 with a (4,8)-connected sqc net is not only thermally stable $(\geq350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , but also exhibits exceptional chemical stability in aqueous solutions including boiling water with a wide $\mathrm{\pH}$ range of 1–11.[52] In recent years, MOFs and COFs have been increasingly explored as photocatalysts to utilize solar energy for many types of reactions. When the organic linkers in the frameworks can absorb light and subsequently transfer electron to the metal center via ligand-localized exciton and charge-separate state, they behave like semiconductors. They can be used as the photocatalysts when appropriate active sites are available in the frameworks. Overall, these basic advantages of MOFs and COFs give rise to the “chemistry of the framework,” where Lewis’ concept of the atom and molecule is extended to the framework in which matter can be further controlled and manipulated for heterogeneous catalysis at mole­ cular level.[36]
## 2.3. Typical Characterization Techniques
X-ray powder diffraction (XRD), physisorption, thermogravimetry, and nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) are the most applied characterization techniques to determine the properties of MOFs and COFs in catalytic applications. Apart from these bulk characterization techniques, more experimental tools are needed to investigate the active sites and provide insight into the reaction mechanism during the catalytic process. Several of such important techniques include singlecrystal X-ray diffraction, infrared (IR) spectroscopy, X-ray absorption spectroscopy, and transmission electron microscopy (TEM). One or two critical examples will be given in the following sections to show the importance of such tools in characterization of the active sites of MOFs and COFs.
## 2.3.1. Single-Crystal X-Ray Diffraction
Single-crystal X-ray diffraction has been routinely used to determinate the structure of the crystalline framework, which can reveal detailed information about the internal lattice of crystalline substances, including precise atomic coordinates, bond lengths, bond angles, and atomic occupancies.[53] For example, Figure 2 shows that the single-crystal X-ray diffraction analysis provides the unequivocal evidence of the sulfate units bounded to $\mathrm{zr}$ centers of MOF-808 [ $\mathsf{M O F}{-}808{-}2.5\mathsf{S O}_{4}$ : $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{5}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}(\mathrm{BTC}$ $)_{2}(\mathrm{SO}_{4})_{2.5}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2.5}]$ after treating $\mathrm{Zr(IV)}$ MOF-808 [MOF-808-P: $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{5}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}(\mathrm{HCOO})_{5}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}$ BTC: 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylate] with aqueous sulfuric acid to generate its sulfated analog, and the disordered sulfate groups of M $\mathrm{OF}{-}808{-}2.5\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ have a chemical occupancy of $0.2.^{[54]}$ In another example, singlecrystal X-ray diffraction results reveal the postsynthetic metalation of triarylphosphine-based $\mathrm{Zr(IV)}$ MOF with Rh or Ir metal complexes, during which Rh coordinates to one phosphine ligand in the MOF and affords single-site mono(phosphine)− Rh catalysts.[55]
## 2.3.2. Infrared Spectroscopy
IR spectroscopy is one of the powerful tools to capture a series of changes on the crystalline frameworks based on the detection of vibrational fingerprints.[56] First, IR spectroscopy can probe the solvent removal process of MOFs and COFs after activation, which results in the open cavity in MOFs and COFs and possibly the open metal sites in MOFs.[57] The open metal sites with coordinatively unsaturated metals in MOFs can serve as isolated active sites for heterogeneous catalysts, which is discussed in Section 3.1. Second, the access of reactant substrate into the frameworks and dynamic reaction process occurring inside the cavities can also be characterized by IR spectroscopy especially the in situ IR spectroscopy, signaling from the interaction of intermediate species in reaction chain with the surface of frameworks. Thus, IR spectroscopy is one of the most useful techniques to understand the structure–activity relationship between the substrate and the catalytic site. For example, IR spectroscopy was successfully applied to reveal the molecular dissociation of $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ adsorbed on MOF-74 to form stable NO and ${\mathrm{NO}}_{3}{}^{-}$ . Such an observation was correlated to strong $\mathrm{NO}_{2}{-}\mathrm{Zn}$ bonding that induces a significant weakening of the $_{\mathrm{N-O}}$ bond and facilitates the decomposition of $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ mole­ cules.[58] Third, IR spectroscopy can provide the evidence of chemical functionalization in COFs and MOFs by organic moieties owing to the existence of signature chemical groups after functionalization. Finally, IR spectroscopy can also be used to check the stability of the crystalline frameworks in different conditions especially in acidic and basic solutions or high temperature, by comparing the change of the vibrational fingerprints before and after process.
## 2.3.3. X-Ray Absorption Spectroscopy
With the accessibility of synchrotron radiation sources, X-ray absorption spectroscopy has become one of the most important techniques to investigate local atomic and molecular structures.[59] Figure 3 shows the principle of X-ray absorption near-edge structure (XANES) and extended X-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) spectroscopy in the region of X-ray absorption spectra. An absorption step is observed in the XANES region, corresponding to the transition of an electron from a core level to an unoccupied orbital or band. EXAFS results from the interference in the scattering process of the photoelectron scattered by surrounding atoms. In summary, XANES gives the fingerprint information of the structure such as the oxidation state and symmetry around the absorber atom, while EXAFS is able to reveal the local coordination environment around the absorber atom such as the number and species of neighboring atoms and their distances from the selected atoms.
Recently, XANES and EXAFS techniques have been extensively used to reveal the structural information of MOF- and
COF-based solid catalysts and particularly structure–activity relationships. For instance, such analysis has provided clear evidence that the removal of water and DMF should not have a direct influence on the coordinative sphere of Zn in MOF-5, revealing the good stability of MOFs during the activation process.[60] In our previous study, EXAFS analysis was used to analyze the local coordination environment of Pt atom upon insertion in MOF253 (Al(OH)(bpydc)) by postsynthetic modification of $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine-based MOF with platinum ions.[61] The experimental Fourier transform spectrum from K3-weighted normalized EXAFS result shows a small peak of $\mathrm{Pt{-}N}$ bond around $1.5\mathrm{~\AA~}$ and a strong peak of $\mathrm{Pt\mathrm{-}C l}$ bond at ${\approx}2.0$ Å. Therefore, it is revealed that the coordination of $\mathrm{Pt}$ complex in MOF-253 is through the formation of $\mathrm{Pt-N}$ bond. An additional peak around $3.6\mathrm{~\AA~}$ was observed, resulting from the short-range noncovalent $\mathrm{Pt\cdotsPt}$ interaction. Phase-shift correction from two single scattering paths between Pt and neighboring $\mathrm{~N~}$ or Cl atoms leads to the fitting of the inverse Fourier transform spectrum of the two peaks. The best-fit results indicate the $\mathrm{Pt-N}$ distance of $1.956\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ and Pt–Cl distance of $2.301\mathrm{~\AA~}$ in good agreement with the data derived from the crystal structure of the similarly coordinated complex.
## 2.3.4. Transmission Electron Microscopy
Despite the use of spectroscopic methods, it is challenging to obtain the spatial distribution of the active sites in fine porous structure owing to the very small length scales of these systems and their complex morphologies. The aberration-corrected scanning transmission electron microscopy (STEM) technique especially the high-angle annular dark-field imaging (HAADF) is the ideal tool to characterize 3D morphologies with atomic resolution. HAADF-STEM can accurately determine the position and distribution of single site active sites within the framework, especially discerning individual heavy atoms. For example, it was confirmed from HAADF-STEM images that the bright spots corresponding to single Pt atoms of less than $0.5\mathrm{nm}$ were uniformly dispersed on covalent triazine framework (CTF).[62] However, as COFs or MOFs mainly contain carbon and other light elements, so the images need to be taken with a very short exposure time as prolonged probe scanning on the samples may cause beam damage and unwanted growth. Recently, Jiang and co-workers also reported the introduction of single Pt atoms into Al-TCPP MOF for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production, where single Pt atoms strongly interact with pyrrolic N atoms in Al-TCPP. HAADFSTEM analysis revealed the existence of abundant Pt single atoms with ultrasmall sizes between 0.1 and $0.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ uniformly dispersed inside Al-TCPP.[63] The aberration-corrected HAADFSTEM image also clearly shows the presence of single Pt atoms and a few Pt clusters when slight Pt aggregation occurs with increasing $\mathrm{Pt}$ content. Combined with the XAS technique, it revealed that the single-atom Pt has been stabilized by nitrogen atoms and almost no (or very few) Pt particles or clusters exist in Al-TCPP since there is only one prominent peak at around $1.5\mathrm{~\AA~}$ from the Pt–N contribution and no significant PtCl $(\approx1.9\mathrm{\AA})$ or PtPt $({\approx}2.7\mathrm{\AA})$ ) bond in Al-TCPP.
## 3. Rational Design of the Diverse Catalytic Sites in Crystalline Frameworks
As the theme of this review article is to discuss the essential roles of MOFs and COFs in terms of directly acting as or influencing the catalytic sites at molecular level, the state-of-the-art accomplishments of rational design of MOF- and COF-based solid catalysts in the current literature are grouped into seven different areas: (i) metalated linkers, ii) metalated moieties anchored on linkers, (iii) organic moieties anchored on linkers, (iv) encapsulated single sites in pores, and (v) metal-modebased active sites in MOFs.
## 3.1. Metalated Linkers
Linker metalation is an effective approach to create catalytically active centers in crystalline frameworks. As mentioned above, in COF chemistry, the modular nature provides a unique platform to incorporate various rigid molecular building blocks or units that can be metalated to form catalytic sites within the framework. Generally, linker metalation in COF structures could be accomplished through bottom-up strategies, which allow for high and homogeneous loading of metal-containing functional groups or catalytic moieties over the frameworks. Table 2 summarizes some typical examples, especially those for heterogeneous catalysis applications. The characteristics of this type of catalytic frameworks, in most reported cases, is a metal cation (e.g., Fe,[64,65] Co,[22,66] Ni,[67] Cu,[67,68] and $\mathrm{Mn}^{[65,69]}\$ embedded in porphyrin-based ligand which serves as singlesite active center. Due to their unique macrocyclic feature, these porphyrin-based building blocks can be incorporated into the organic framework easily by forming interlinkers with rigid units. Such material systems could potentially combine the advantages of both molecular (built-in coordinating units) and heterogeneous (COF skeleton) catalysts, allowing design and manipulation of catalytic centers in a known COF structure.
Jiang and co-workers reported the synthesis of a crystalline 2D-conjugated COF (denoted as CuP-SQ) via the condensation of squaric acid (SQ) and copper(II)5,10,15,20-tetrakis(4- aminophenyl)porphyrin (TAP-CuP) under solvothermal conditions.[68] The resultant COF-SQ COF is highly crystalline, porous, and solvent stable. Moreover, the extended $\pi$ -conjugation structure in the framework effectively improves the light harvesting capacity and lowers the bandgap compared to the monomeric CuP. When being used as a photocatalyst for the activation of molecular oxygen, the resultant CuP-SQ COF system exhibits better catalytic activity compared to monomeric CuP system owning to its higher efficiency in generating the triplet state. In another related example, Chang and co-workers constructed catalytic COF series comprising cobalt porphyrins as catalytic centers for electrocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction.[22] A model COF structure (COF-367-Co) was synthesized by means of the imine condensation of 5,10,15,20-tetrakis(4-aminophenyl)porphinato cobalt [Co(TAP)] and $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -biphenyldicarbaldehyde. In such a COF structure, the building blocks are catalytic cobalt porphyrin units that are linked by organic struts through imine bonds (Figure 4a). Electrochemical studies revealed that the COF-367-Co catalyst exhibits a high Faradaic efficiency of $91\%$ and turnover numbers (TONs) up to 48 000 at an applied potential of −0.67 V (vs RHE) for electrocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction in water, which are much higher than the activity of the molecular cobalt complex analog [Co(TAP)]. Moreover, the catalyst performance could be further optimized by introducing building-block heterogeneity (e.g., copper porphyrin) through a multivariate strategy. X-ray absorption analysis showed that the COF-367-Co and the bimetallic $\mathrm{COF}{-}367{\cdot}\mathrm{Co}(10\%)$ catalysts exhibit an additional pre-edge feature which is not present in [Co(TAP)] (Figure 4b). The COF environment could furnish a catalytic cobalt center with a more delocalized electronic structure, thus facilitating the electrocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction.
In another recent important work, Johnson et  al. constructed zwitteronic MOFs by assembling anionic secondary building blocks $([\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{CO}_{2})_{4}]^{-})$ with cationic metalloporphyrin-based organic through solvothermal reactions.[70] The change of coordination environment in the porphyrin metal center resulted from anion metathesis was analyzed by using singlecrystal X-ray diffraction, UV–vis spectra, and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS ) spectra. An attractive property from the resulting unique structure is the complete internal charge separation which could effectively prevent the potential binding between the counteranion to the cationic metal center and thus change their electronic properties. As demonstrated, the zwitterionic MOFs display better catalytic activities for a few representative organic transformations, $[2+1]$ cycloisomerization of enzymes, $[3~+~2]$ cycloaddition of aziridines and alkenes, and $[4~+~2]$ hetero-Diels−Alder cycloaddition of aldehydes with dienes (Figure 4c). The porphyrin metal centers showed enhanced Lewis acid catalytic activities due to the modulation of their electronic properties such as the orbital energies and electronic density distribution. Such changes can influence the activation energy of the substrates and may also lower the activation barrier of subsequent reaction steps.
Apart from porphyrins and metalloporphyrins, some other complexes or analogs, such as metal phthalocyanine,[64] bipyridine and phenylpyridine complexes,[71] salen complexes,[72,73] and N-heterocyclic carbene gold(I),[74] have also been reported as building units to construct catalytically active sites in metalfunctionalized frameworks. In an example based on salen complex building blocks, The $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ ions of Zn(salen)-based COFs can be postsynthetic exchanged with a variety of metal $\mathrm{M}=\mathrm{V},$ Mn, Fe, and Co) ions to generate $Z\mathrm{nM}$ (salen) complexes in Zn(salen)-based CCOFs for heterogeneous asymmetric catalysis (Figure 5).[72] The exchanged COFs maintain high crystallinity and porosity and was used as efficient and recyclable heterogeneous catalysts for asymmetric cyanation of aldehydes, Diels–Alder reaction, alkene epoxidation, epoxide ring opening, and related sequential reactions with up to $97\%$ ee. This work provides a new approach to achieve high chemical stability, catalytic activity, and enantioselectivity in COFs and can promote the design of COFs from privileged chiral ligands/catalysts that will display interesting chirality properties.
## 3.2. Metalated Moieties Anchored on Linkers
Anchoring metalated moieties on organic linkers is a commonly used grafting technique to functionalize MOF or COF structures with additional active species. In this type of catalytic frameworks, organic linkers (or ligands) are decorated with foreign metal-containing groups (e.g., metal complexes or inorganic groups) that could introduce a catalytically active function onto the parent framework. With the rapid development of synthesis chemistry and functionalization strategies, a variety of catalytic metalated moieties have been anchored on various frameworks. Some representative examples taken from publications form different research groups are summarized in Table 3.
Direct solvothermal synthesis, i.e., bottomup approach, can be used to introduce singlesite, molecular-style catalysts into MOFs. In a research work from Lin and co-workers, catalytically competent Ir, Re, and Ru complexes were incorporated into UiO-67 frameworks $\mathrm{[Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}(b p d c)_{6}}$ bpdc $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ para-biphenyldicarboxylic acid] via a so-called “mix-andmatch” synthetic strategy.[75] In this synthesis, the matching between the lengths of bpdc and metalloligands allows the construction of highly crystalline UiO-67 frameworks doped with metalloligands (Figure 6). These metal complex derivatized MOFs $(\mathrm{Ir/Rr/Ru}$ doped UiO-67) are isostructural with the parent UiO-67 framework as confirmed by their XRD patterns. The modified UiO-67 materials were demonstrated as efficient catalysts for various applications including carbon dioxide reduction, water oxidation, and organic photocatalysis depending on the specific metalloligand inserted. Using a similar “mix-and-match” strategy, [Ru(dcbpy) $(\log9)_{2}]^{2+}$ $(\mathrm{dcbpy}\:=\:2,2^{\prime}$ -bipyridyl- $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylic acid, bpy $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine) and $\mathrm{Pt}(\mathrm{dcbpy})\mathrm{Cl}_{2},$ a molecular photosensitizer and proton reduction molecular catalyst, respectively, were successfully incorporated into UiO-67 frameworks by Hou et al.[76] The successful incorporation of Ru and Pt complexes into MOFs was verified by using FT-IR, UV–vis, and XPS spectra. The as-formed $\mathrm{Ru-Pt}@\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}67$ MOF system allows the photosensitizer and the reduction catalyst arranged with close spatial proximity for efficient charge transfer, leading to a significantly improved hydrogen evolution activity upon visible light irradiation. It is worth mentioning that the successful incorporation of metalloligands in MOFs through direct synthesis has been mainly limited to kinetically inert metals (e.g., second- or thirdrow organometallic species).[75–77] Those thermodynamically more labile complexes may not be amenable to direct installation in this way because they may not be sufficiently stable to remain intact under solvothermal conditions. Moreover, some functional groups, if added to the monomers or building blocks during presynthetic functionalization, may hamper further polymerization or the construction of crystalline frameworks. It should be noted that such direct synthetic pathways although allow the access to MOF materials modified by desirable metalated moieties, the precise control of the location of these metalated moieties in most cases is usually difficult to achieve due to complicated reaction conditions.
Besides the aforementioned bottom-up strategies based on direct synthesis, a different approach that has also been commonly adopted to anchor desired metalated moiety on organic linkers is postsynthetic functionalization (i.e., top-down approach) of a preformed framework. Two different postsynthetic functionalization strategies have been reported for the incorporation or grafting of metalated moieties into the existing frameworks to create catalytic sites. In the first strategy, catalytically active frameworks can be formed by incorporating or grafting a presynthesized metal-containing catalytic group (e.g., complexes or metalloligands) into the parent frameworks by reacting with the organic linkers. In a related effort, Feng and co-workers incorporated iron hydrogenase active sites into a zirconium–porphyrin-based MOF $(\mathrm{ZrPF})$ .[78] In this system, the MOF was first constructed from a tetrakis[(4-carboxyphenyl) porphyrin]zinccomplex (ZnTCPP) and $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ via a solvothermal reaction. One important feature of this MOF structure is that the axial coordination sites in the ZnTCPP metalloligand remains open. This allows for introduction of the $\mathrm{[Fe}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}]$ complex through the axial coordination of the pyridine nitrogen to zinc porphyrin (Figure 7). A second important feature of the resulting [FeFe] $\ @\mathrm{ZrPF}$ system is that the ZnTCPP units are photoactive and can function as a photosensitizer that is in close proximity to the [FeFe] catalytic active site, which facilitates electron transfer with the avoidance of using an electron mediator to transfer electrons from the photosensitizer to the catalytic centers. The incorporation of the $\mathrm{[Fe}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}]$ binuclear complex into the $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{ZrPF}}$ framework was demonstrated by FT-IR and thermogravimetric analysis. As seen from the photocatalytic study, the resultant [FeFe] $\textcircled{a}Z\mathrm{rPF}$ system is active for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution under visible light irradiation in aqueous solution with ascorbic acid as an electron donor. Similarly, various [FeFe]-based hydrogenase active sites have been incorporated into other MOFs such as $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66^{[79]}$ and $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101(\mathrm{Cr})^{[80]}$ for photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution. In the case of anchoring the preformed complexes on linkers, Yu and Cohen applied a postsynthetic modification method to incorporate cyclometalated Ir complexes into a $\mathrm{UiO-}67\mathrm{-}\mathrm{bpy}_{0.25}$ framework.[81] The successful incorporation of Ir complexes relies on the coordination reaction between bpy units in the MOF structure and the foreign Ir complexes. The resulting Ir-doped $\mathrm{UiO-}67\mathrm{-}\mathrm{bpy}_{0.25}$ MOF systems were used as photocatalysts for trifluoroethylation reaction of styrene with high yields under visible light irradiation. Most importantly, when compared to the homogeneous Ir complexes, the Ir-doped $\mathrm{UiO-67.bpy_{0.25}M O F}$ catalysts favor the formation of the desired hydroxy trifluoroethyl products, while suppressing dimerization of benzyl radicals that results in undesirable by-products, owing to the confined space within the MOF pores.
In a strategy different from affixing a presynthesized reactive group onto the frameworks, postsynthetic functionalization is based on metalation reactions. Postsynthetic metalation leads to the formation of catalytic centers at the organic linker via coordination of the available functional group in the organic linker to active metal species inserted. This strategy has been widely applied in frameworks comprising pyridine or derivative units, amine groups, thiocatechol groups, or phosphine ligands, which have high affinity for metal ions. With such a postsynthetic strategy, a variety of metal precursors, such as Pt,[62,82] Pd,[83–87] $\begin{array}{r}{\mathrm{{Ru},}}\end{array}$ [88–90] Rh,[91–93] Ir,[93] Au,[94] $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}$ ,[95] Ni,[96] Co,[97,91] $\mathrm{Cr}$ ,[98] Ga,[98] and $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ ,[99–102] based species, have been introduced into MOFs or COFs to create catalytically active sites. For example, our group reported the construction of a bi-functional MOF (MOF-253-Pt) photocatalyst by immobilizing a platinum complex in $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine-based MOF (MOF-253).[61] In this MOF framework, the accessible $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine units provide rich uncoordinated nitrogen sites for metalation functionalization to create catalytic centers. MOF$253{\cdot}\mathrm{Pt}$ was obtained by postmodification of MOF-253 with cis$\mathrm{Pt}(\mathrm{DMSO})_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ species. The coordination of $\mathrm{Pt}(\log)\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ complex within the parent MOF structure (Figure 8a) was confirmed by XRD, BET, and extended EXAFS spectroscopic analyses. The resultant MOF-253-Pt is active for photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution under visible light without any additional catalyst and photosensitizer. The photocatalytic activity of MOF-253-Pt for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution is much higher than that of $\mathrm{Pt}(\log\mathrm{ydc})\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ complex. The enhancement in photocatalytic performance is attributed to the interaction of shortly separated $\mathrm{Pt\cdotsPt}$ pairs and more efficient electron transfer and separation within the modified framework, as well as higher stability of the in situ formed $\mathrm{Pt}(\log)\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ complex within the MOF. Analogously, Cohen and co-workers reported the postsynthetic metalation of a robust $\mathrm{Zr(IV)}$ -MOF with open bpy metal-chelating linkers to achieve isolated $\mathrm{Mn(bpy)-(CO)_{3}B r}$ sites in the MOF.[95] With the combination of $\mathrm{[Ru(dmb)_{3}]^{2+}}$ $[\mathrm{d}\mathrm{m}\mathrm{b}=4,4^{\prime}$ -dimethyl- $^{\cdot2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine) as the photosensitizer and 1-benzyl-1,4-dihydronicotinamide as the sacrificial electron donor, the resulting UiO-67-Mn(bpy) $(\mathrm{CO})_{3}\mathrm{Br}$ was shown as highly active and selective $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction to formate under visible light. In a recent example, Huang and co-workers modified an Al-based MOF (Al-ATA MOF) by incorporating $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}$ cations into the framework through coordination to the amino groups.[96] The Al-ATA MOF consists of $\mathrm{AlO_{4}(O H)_{4}}$ octahedra linked by carboxylate groups of 2-aminoterephthalates $(\mathrm{ATA}^{2-})$ . The incorporated Ni atoms in the modified Al-ATA-Ni MOF are in an oxidation state of around $^{2+}$ and are octahedrally coordinated as confirmed by XANES and EXAFS analyses. When used as a photocatalyst for overall water splitting, the benzene ring of $\mathrm{ATA}^{2-}$ acts as the active site for $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ evolution while the anchored $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ complex within the framework functions as the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution site. Moreover, IRMOF-3 was postsynthetically modified by salicylaldehyde via $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ groups which was then coordinated with $\mathrm{NaAuCl}_{4}$ to form the $\mathrm{Au(III)}$ Schiff base complex on the pore wall of IRMOF-3. This heterogeneous catalyst exhibits much higher catalytic performance for coupling and hydrogenation reactions, compared with oxide-supported gold and homogeneous gold salt/complexes, because its excellent ability to stabilize cationic Au(III) species and $100\%$ utility of gold active sites.[103] Recently, Lin and co-workers designed a series of mesoporous chiral MOFs with the framework formula $\mathrm{[LCu}_{2}(\mathrm{solvent})_{2}]$ (L is a chiral tetracarboxylate ligand derived from 1,1′-bi-2-naphthol) and then postsynthetic grafted the $\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{O}^{i}\mathrm{Pr})_{4}$ complex onto the chiral dihydroxy groups.[104] $\mathsf{M O F/T i(O^{i}P r)_{4}}$ was demonstrated as highly active asymmetric catalysts for converting aromatic aldehydes into chiral secondary alcohols. It was found that tuning the size of the channels alters the diffusion rates of the organic substrates.
The same strategy can also be applied to COFs to create catalytic active frameworks. For instance, a robust water oxidation electrocatalyst based on Co-modified bipyridinecontaining COF (Co-TpBpy) (Figure 8b) was reported by Aiyappa et  al.[97] A simple postmodification of the bipyridine-based COFs (TpBpy) by soaking the pristine COF material in methanolic cobalt acetate solution produces Co-TpBpy. The formation of the complex between cobalt and bipyridin unit in the framework was confirmed by XRD, FTIR, and UV–vis studies. Benefiting from the synergistic effect of the inherent porosity and the presence of Co-based coordinating units in the COF skeleton, Co-TpBpy exhibits efficient electrocatalytic activity toward water oxidation in phosphate buffer under neutral pH conditions.
## 3.3. Organic Moieties Anchored on Linkers
Anchoring proper organic moieties on the linkers is another effective postsynthetic modification strategy for imparting desired chemical functionalities and exploiting new applications in MOF or COF structures. Certain organic functional groups themselves can serve as active sites for catalytic applications. In addition, some of them can be further functionalized by reactive centers such as metal complexes or chiral basic groups to introduce new active sites onto the parent framework. The fact of increasingly more recent achievements in this area highlights the advantages of this strategy for constructing catalytic frameworks, as summarized in Table 4.
Some organic functional groups can be introduced into MOF or COF structures through bottom-up strategy by selecting proper building blocks. For instance, Gascon and coworkers constructed two MOFs bearing sulfonic acid moieties, namely $\mathrm{[Cr_{3}O F(H_{2}O)_{2}(b d c\mathrm{-}S O_{3}H)_{3}]\cdot}n H_{2}O$ $(\mathrm{HSO_{3}}–\mathrm{MIL}–101(\mathrm{Cr}))$ and $\mathrm{HSO}_{3^{-}}\mathrm{MOF}(\mathrm{Zr})$ , via direct synthesis processes.[105] The acid catalytic activity of these two MOFs was evaluated in the esterification of acetic acid and $n$ -butanol, against that of the benchmark catalyst Amberlyst-15 (A-15). The crystallinity and reusability of these MOFs can be retained after four catalytic regeneration cycles indicating their potential chemical stability. It was revealed after reactivation under acidic conditions, sulfonic ester formation can be reversed. Chen et al. also reported the use of $\mathrm{HSO}_{3}–\mathrm{MIL}–101(\mathrm{Cr})$ as a heterogeneous catalyst for transfer hydrogenation of imines by using Hantzsch ester as the reductant.[106] They demonstrated that the ${\mathrm{Cr}}^{3+}$ node and $-\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ group function cooperatively in promoting the catalytic reaction, resulting in an enhanced reactivity as compared to its homogeneous analog. Recently, MOFs and COFs containing sulfonate groups as solid acid catalysts in biobased conversion have drawn increasingly significant attention.[107–110] An MIL-101-based crystalline framework containing sulfonic acid groups was synthesized by using 2-sulfoterephthalate instead of the unsubstituted terephthalate in MIL-101, during which sulfonic groups on the pore surface act as strong Brønsted acid sites to catalyze cellulose hydrolysis into mono- and disaccharides with high durability.[107] Zhao and co-workers also hydrothermally synthesized two sulfonated MOFs named $\mathrm{NUS-}6(\mathrm{Zr})$ and NUS-6(Hf) from the mixtures of BDC- $\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{Na}$ with either $\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}$ or $\mathrm{HfCl}_{4}$ .[108] Structural analysis revealed from synchrotron PXRD data that the hierarchical porosity of NUS-6 is a result of missing linkers and clusters of the parental UiO-66 framework. These unique features make NUS-6 to be highly efficient and selective solid acid catalysts for dehydration of fructose to 5-hydroxymethylfurfural during which NUS-6(Hf) exhibited better reaction kinetics and chemoselectivity with quantitative fructose conversion and a 5-hydroxymethylfurfural yield of $98\%$ . Zhao and co-workers also developed sulfonated 2D crystalline COF TFP-DABA from 1,3,5-triformylphloroglucinol and 2,5-diaminobenzenesulfonic acid through Schiff base condensation reaction and then irreversible enol-to-keto tautomerization.[109] And sulfonated COF TFP-DABA served as highly efficient solid acid catalyst for fructose conversion with remarkable yields $(97\%$ for 5-hydroxymethylfurfural and $65\%$ for 2,5-diformylfuran), good chemoselectivity, and good recyclability. A series of sulfonic acid-functionalized MOFs (MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr})$ $\mathrm{\small{[MIL-101(Cr)-SO_{3}H]}}$ , UIO-66(Zr) [UIO-66(Zr)- $\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}]$ , and MIL-53(Al) [MIL-53(Al)- $\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}]_{\mathrm{\Omega}}$ ) were prepared by postsynthetic modification of the organic linkers with chlorosulfonic acid for catalytic fructose to 5-hydroxymethylfurfural transformation.[110] A high HMF yield of up to $90\%$ with a full fructose conversion was obtained with MIL-101(Cr)- $\mathrm{\nabla{\cdot}}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ as the catalyst owing to the higher extent of $\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ grafting. The sulfonated NUS-10 COF was also prepared by condensation reactions between 1,3,5-triformylphloroglucinol (TFP) with 2,5-diamino-benzene1,4-disulfonic acid, which exhibited high intrinsic proton conductivity as high as $3.96\times10^{-2}\mathrm{~S~cm^{-1}}$ with long-term stability at ambient temperature and $97\%$ relative humidity.[111] After this COF was blended with nonconductive polyvinylidene fluoride, it affords a series of mixed-matrix membranes with a proton conductivity of up to $1.58\times10^{-2}10^{-2}$ $\mathrm{\sfScm^{-1}}$ and a low activation energy of $0.21\ \mathrm{eV},$ suggesting the Grotthuss mechanism for proton conduction. Imidazole-based TPB-DMTP-COF also achieved proton conductivities that are 2–4 orders of magnitude higher than those of microporous and nonporous polymers.[112] Wu and co-workers studied the structure–photocatalytic activity relation with an isoreticular series of UiO-66 MOFs containing different ligand substitutions.[113]
UiO-66-X ${\left(\mathrm{X}=\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}\right.}$ , $\mathsf{N O}_{2}$ , and Br) MOFs were synthesized by replacing the original linker, terephthalic acid $\mathrm{(H}_{2}B D C)$ , with equivalent molar amounts of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ , $\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{N}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ , and $\mathtt{B r-H}_{2}\mathtt{B D C}$ , respectively. It was found that the electronic property of the ligand substituents plays an important role in the photocatalytic activity of the MOFs. UiO-66 bearing electrondonating substituent (i.e., $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ ) is more reactive than that with electron-withdrawing substituents (i.e., $\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ and Br).
In MOF and COF chemistry, postsynthetic strategies have also been effectively used to introduce foreign organic moieties into the parent framework structures, including (i) solvent-assisted ligand incorporation, (ii) solvent-assisted linker exchange, and (iii) organic reaction between the functional group on linker and foreign organic species. Depending on the bond lability and overall stability of a given foreign organic moiety and the parent framework, a different approach needs to be used.
Solvent-assisted ligand incorporation strategy involves replacing the labile, nonstructural inorganic ligands in parent MOF frameworks with new organic ligands. It has been widely applied to modify MOF structures for desired catalytic applications. In most reported cases, metal nodes serve as the anchoring sites for grafting new organic moieties. Farha and co-workers demonstrated that NU-1000, a $\mathrm{Zr}$ -based MOF composed of $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}(\mu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{O})_{4}(\mu_{3}{-}\mathrm{OH})_{4}(-\mathrm{OH})_{4}(-\mathrm{OH}_{2})_{4}$ nodes and tetratopic1,3,6,8-tetrakis( $\dot{p}$ -benzoate)pyrene $(\mathrm{TBAP}_{\gamma}^{4-})$ linkers, can efficiently incorporate carboxylic-acid-based alkyl groups.[114] In NU-1000 structure, the eight terminal ${-}\mathrm{OH}/{-}\mathrm{OH}_{2}$ groups were accessible for chemical modification. This allowed the insertion of carboxylate-derived functionalities into the parent NU-1000 framework via an acid-based reaction between a carboxylic-acid-containing functional group and a pair of terminal hydroxide ligands of the $\ensuremath{\mathrm{zr}}_{6}$ node. Moreover, these carboxylic-acid-containing functional groups present opportunities for secondary postsynthetic modification through further reactions such as imine condensation, pyridine quaternization, and click chemistry.[115] With a similar methodology, the same group further demonstrated that ligand incorporation of NU-1000 can be accomplished with phosphonate-terminated ligands, which possess stronger capability to coordinate $\mathrm{{Zr}(I V)}$ than carboxylates, resulting in modified NU-1000 materials with enhanced chemical stability.[116] Zhou and co-workers studied the linker effect on the breathing behaviors and catalytic activity on a flexible $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOF system $(\mathrm{PCN-}700{\cdot}\mathrm{Me}_{2})$ ).[117] In $\mathrm{.\PCN{\cdot}{700}{\cdot}M e_{2}}$ , each $\ensuremath{\mathrm{zr}}_{6}$ cluster is coordinated to eight $\mathrm{Me}_{2}$ -BPDC linkers $(\mathrm{Me}_{2}.$ BPDC $\mathit{\Theta}=\ 2{,}2^{\prime}$ -dimethylbiphenyl- $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylate) and eight terminal ${-\mathrm{OH^{-}/H_{2}O}}$ groups. Four different linear linkers including fumarate (FA), 1,4-benzenedicarboxylate (BDC), 2,6-napthalene dicarboxylate (NDC), and $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -biphenyldicarboxylate (BPDC), were installed in PCN-700 framework, respectively, by exposing the $\mathrm{PCN-}700{\cdot}\mathrm{Me}_{2}$ crystal to a DMF solution of the linkers at $75~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . Due to the different lengths, linker installation results in four different kinds of daughter MOFs with varying degree of unit cell dimension change (denoted as PCN-700-FA, PCN-700-BPDC, PCN-700-NDC, and PCN-700-BDC, respectively), analyzed by single-crystal X-ray diffraction (Figure 9). The installation of FA linker squeezes the structure due to its shorter length (4.95 Å) compared to the distance between adjacent $\ensuremath{\mathrm{zr}}_{6}$ clusters in pristine PCN-700- ${\mathrm{Me}}_{2}{\mathrm{MOF}}$ (6.98 Å), whereas the NDC and BPDC open up the structures owing to their longer lengths. Different from these two cases, the introduction of BDC linker does not change the unit cell parameter significantly. These modified $\mathrm{PCN}{\cdot}700{\cdot}\mathrm{Me}_{2}$ with different linkers display different catalytic activities for cycloaddition reaction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ with epoxides. The activity trend follows PCN-700-BPDC $>$ PCN-700-NDC $>$ PCN-700-BDC $>$ PCN-700-FA, which was ascribed to different diffusion rate of substrates and different Lewis acid sites (the ${-\mathrm{OH^{-}/H_{2}O}}$ groups along the $\boldsymbol{\mathscr{c}}$ -direction) of the catalysts.
Linker exchange is another alternative technology to the de novo synthesis of catalytically active MOF materials. This strategy involves the partial or complete replacement of structural ligands in a parent MOF with alternative linkers. Linker exchange is initiated by exposing a parent MOF to a concentrated solution of a new linker. The degree of exchange depends on the lability of the metalligand bond in the parent MOF structure. As a result, the concentration of the new linker in the daughter MOF can vary anywhere from $1\%$ to $100\%$ . Importantly, the daughter MOF generally retains the topology of the parent framework. Thus, the linker exchange method provides a way to synthesize new MOFs through MOF-to-MOF transformation or to introduce new chemical functionalities in the parent MOFs which may not be accessible de novo. The first reported examples of linker exchange on 3D MOFs came from Choe and co-workers.[118] They focused on pillared-paddle wheel
MOFs and showed that a previously unobtainable MOF can be formed. Following this pioneer work, the linker exchange concept was soon extended to other classes of MOFs to produce new catalytically active MOFs. One such example was reported by Hupp and co-workers.[119] By using ZIF-8 as the template and solvent-assisted linker exchange as a postsynthetic modification technique, they obtained a ZIF of sodalite topology with up to $85\%$ exchange of 2-methyl-imidazolate linkers for unsubstituted imidazolate within ZIF-8. The structure of the linkerexchange-derived MOF materials was confirmed through $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR and single-crystal X-ray diffraction. The linker-exchangederived MOF materials retain the porosity of the parent ZIF-8, but offer a larger size cutoff for uptake of molecular guest. Furthermore, the treatment of the linker-exchange-derived MOF with $n$ -butyl lithium leads to a Brønsted base catalyst for conjugate addition of alcohol to an $\alpha,\beta$ -unsaturated ketone, which is not possible to achieve by the parent ZIF-8 material. In another example, the linker exchange technique was used by Takaishi et al. to incorporate an active pillar into robust porphyrinic MOFs (RPMs).[120] RPMs belongs to a class of frameworks that are constructed from bytetratopictetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl) porphyrin linkers, ditopicdipyridyl-porphyrin pillars, and dinuclear transition-metal-ion nodes. The formation of the RPM conners requires zinc source. It is challenging to incorporate a metal-free porphyrin pillar de novo, as the conditions for the solvothermal reaction of RPMs promote the metalation of the porphyrin with the zinc species. Moreover, the introduction of the metal species such as ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ , $\mathbb{A}^{3+}$ , and $\mathrm{Sn^{4+}}$ could not be realized due to the solubility problem and formation of amorphous phases instead of the desirable product. In this case, dipyridylporphyrin $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ (Zn-dipy) struts are successfully replaced by $\mathrm{~\bf~M~}^{2}.$ -dipy $(\mathbf{M}^{2}\ =\ 2\mathrm{H}(+)$ , Al(III), and $\mathrm{Sn}(\mathrm{IV}))$ via solventassisted linker exchange, forming crystalline solid solutions of $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{Zn}_{1-x}\mathrm{M}_{x})$ -RPM in variable ratios. As demonstrated in the linker-exchange-derived ZnAl-RPM sample, it was proved to be an efficient Lewis acid catalyst for epoxide ring opening.
An important breakthrough is the use of linker exchange strategy for COF-to-COF transformation. Recent work by Zhao and co-workers demonstrated that the linker exchange method can be used to construct 2D COFs with complicated topological structures.[121] In this case, a 2D triple-pore COF, TP-COF-BZ, was first synthesized by means of the condensation reaction of $[1,1^{\prime};3^{\prime},1^{\prime\prime}$ -terphenyl]- $3,3^{\prime\prime},5,5^{\prime\prime}$ -tetracarbaldehyde with benzidine. Further reaction of TP-COF-BZ with 1,4-diaminobenzene under solvothermal condition leads to the formation of a new COF structure, TP-COF-DAB, via in situ replacement of the benzidine linkers in the starting MOF structure with foreign 1,4-diaminobenzene linkers, demonstrated by studies of FT-IR and $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ NMR spectra. It was the first time that in situ COFto-COF transformation via heterogeneous linker exchange has been achieved, which might offer a new avenue for modification and functionalization of COFs via ligand exchange. Further development of solvent-assisted ligand-exchange strategies should focus on the precise control of reaction active sites and the extent, which allow the rational design of desirable functionalities in the target MOF or COF materials. In order to precisely explore the ligand-exchange strategies, MOF-5 was selected as a model system to examine the ligand exchange process with benzene-2,3,5,6- $\cdot\mathrm{d}_{4}$ -1,4-dicarboxylic acid $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC-d}_{4})$ as probed ligand.[122] Raman spectra showed that the exchanged ligand is concentrated at the edges of the crystal and decreases in concentration with crystal depth, resulting in a core–shell arrangement, because of the slow diffusion of the carboxylic acid ligand into the pores of the MOF. Examination of ligand exchange in UMCM-8 and single crystals of UiO-66 shows a similar trend.
Postsynthetic modification via organic transformation on the linker is also a powerful strategy for functionalizing MOFs and COFs. It provides a rapid synthetic pathway to introduce new functionalities and can even lead to functionalities otherwise inaccessible. Some recent studies on $\mathrm{Zr}$ -UiO-based MOFs have highlighted the feasibility of postsynthetic modification on 2,2-bypyridine units to impart/optimize their properties and functionalities.[123,124] Du and co-workers applied a nondestructive postsynthetic modification method through N-quaternination of pyridine sites to tune the physical and chemical properties of UiO-67-bpy MOF.[123] Postsynthetic N-quaternination of pyridine site can be accomplished by heating UiO-67-bpy in THF and iodomethane at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $72\mathrm{{h}}$ . The successful methylation of the N-pyridine sites in UiO-67-bpy was revealed by $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR and FT-IR measurements. N-quaternization changes the electronic structure of the organic linker by turning the pristine neutral to cationic framework. The optical absorption of the resultant UiO-67-bpy-Me is significantly redshifted to up to around $800\ \mathrm{nm}$ compared with $350~\mathrm{nm}$ of the parent UiO67-bpy. This makes the new MOF a more efficient catalyst for photodegradation of methyl orange.
Organic linkers in COFs also offer the potential for introducing catalytically active groups by means of organic transformation reactions. Jiang and co-workers developed a chiral COF for heterogeneous asymmetric organocatalysis.[125] During the synthesis, an intermediate $[\mathrm{HC}\equiv\mathrm{C}]_{x}$ -TPB-DMTP-COF was first constructed via a three-component condensation reaction using 2,5-bis(2-propynyloxy)terephthalaldehyde, 2,5-dimethoxyterephthalaldehyde, and 1,3,5-tri-(4-aminophenyl)benzene as the starting materials. The intermediate $[\mathrm{HC}\exists\mathrm{c}]_{x}$ -TPB-DMTP-COF was further converted into chiral organocatalytic [(s)- $\cdot\mathrm{Py}\mathbf{\}_{x}$ -TPBDMTP-COFs[(s)-Py $\mathbf{\xi}=\mathbf{\xi}(s)$ -pyrrolidine] via a quantitative azide– ethynyl click reaction (Figure 10). The successful installation of chiral organocatalytic sites on the mother COF structure was confirmed by using powder XRD and FT-IR analysis. In this modified COF structure, chiral and catalytic (s)-Py sites are randomly anchored onto the channel walls through triazole rings. Moreover, the electron-donating methoxy groups anchoring on the channel walls reinforce the interlayer interaction which leads to the improved stability compared to the counterpart without methoxy groups on the walls. As demonstrated, the resulting chiral COFs function as high-performance catalysts for promoting asymmetric $\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}$ bond formation in water under ambient conditions. The reactants from water phase can be accumulated in the open channels where the reaction takes place over the chiral centers and organocatalytic sites.
## 3.4. Encapsulated Single Sites in Pores
The well-defined pore structure of MOFs or COFs allows the incorporation of single sites into the crystalline frameworks. The tunable pore size facilitates the selection of substrate and/ or product molecules in catalytic reactions to achieve high selectivity. Table 5 lists the typical examples of single site catalysts encapsulated in pores of MOFs[92,126–135] or COFs[62,100,136–140] for catalytic applications.
Rh supported on $\mathrm{MIL}{-}101{-}\mathrm{SO}_{3}$ is significantly more efficient than that over Rh supported on ZJU-28. One critical factor is the large pore size of MIL- $\cdot101{\cdot}{\mathrm{SO}}_{3}.$ which facilitates transport of reactants and products during the reaction. However, the leaching of Rh from MIL- $\cdot101..\mathrm{SO}_{3}$ occurred but not from ZJU28. This work demonstrates the possibility of using MOFs as support materials to modulate both the activity and selectivity of the encapsulated metal complex catalysts.
A “ship-in-a-bottle” synthetic strategy was used to rationally encapsulate $[\mathsf{C o}^{\mathrm{II}}(\mathrm{TPA})\mathsf{C l l}][\mathsf{C l}]$ molecular complex into the cages of MIL- $125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ for visible light driven $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution, characterized by UV–vis spectra, photoluminescence emission spectra, and HAADF-STEM image.[131] Typically, the flexible TPA ligands are able to diffuse into the MOF cages, followed by the introduction of ${\mathrm{Co}}(\mathrm{II})$ cations to form the ${\mathrm{Co}}(\mathrm{II})$ complex. In the ${\mathrm{Co}}(\mathrm{II})\ @\mathrm{MIL}\cdot125{\cdot}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}$ composite, the photoelectrons generated from $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ photosensitizer are transferred to the ${\mathrm{Co}}(\mathrm{II})$ complex that acts as the cocatalyst for reducing protons to $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . Thus, confining the molecular catalyst in the cage of photosensitizers greatly facilitates the spatial charge separation and enhances the photocatalytic efficiency. In addition, the ${\mathrm{Co}}(\mathrm{II})\ @\mathrm{MIL}\cdot125{\cdot}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}$ photocatalyst is readily recyclable. Similarly, this method was used to encapsulate large metal complex molecules beyond the aperture size of the nanospace of MOFs. One example is shown in Figure 11b for the self-assembly of metal and ligand in MIL-101.[132] $\ensuremath{\mathrm{~\textrm~{~~}~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{M}_{6}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{L}_{4}}$ is a hollow octahedral complex with a diagonal Pd–Pd distance of $2.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ , in which M stands for $\mathrm{(en)Pd(NO_{3})_{2}}$ and L for 1,3,5-tris(4-pyridyl)-2,4,6-triazine. As the pore windows of MIL-101(Cr) are smaller than $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}.$ , the migration and leaching of $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ could be effectively prohibited if it can be successfully encapsulated. During the “ship-in-a-bottle” synthesis, $\mathrm{Pd}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}$ was first incorporated into the hydrophilic MIL-101(Cr) pores by capillary force, and L was then drawn into the pores to form $\ensuremath{\mathrm{~\mathbf~{~M~}~}}_{6}\ensuremath{\mathrm{L}_{4}}$ via the selfassembly process. Remarkably, the encapsulated $\mathrm{M}_{6}\mathrm{L}_{4}$ in MIL$101(\mathrm{Cr})$ presents much higher activity and selectivity $95\%$ conversion and $98\%$ selectivity) for oxidation of benzyl alcohol to benzaldehyde, compared to the pristine $\ensuremath{\mathrm{~\textrm~{~~}~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{M}_{6}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{L}_{4}}$ ( $60\%$ conversion and $45\%$ selectivity). The stability of the encapsulated $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}_{6}\ensuremath{\mathrm{L}_{4}}$ is also much higher. The use of MOF is thus critical in preventing the aggregation and leaching of molecular catalysts via the confinement effect.
Polyoxometalates (POMs), as a class of discrete nanometric molecular clusters composed of earth-abundant elements, are particularly attractive as potential catalysts. With properly chosen MOFs with large window openings, various POMs have been successfully encapsulated in MOFs. A simple and effective charge-assisted self-assembly process was developed to in situ encapsulate the Dawson-type polyoxoanions $[\mathrm{P}_{2}\mathrm{W}_{18}\mathrm{O}_{62}]^{6-}$ inside a porous and cationic MOF built from $[\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}]^{2+}$ - derived dicarboxylate ligands and $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}(\mu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{O})_{4}(\mu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{OH})_{4}$ secondary building units, characterized by powder XRD pattern, UV–vis spectra, and TEM image. The resultant composite photocatalyst gives rise to efficient $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution activity under visible light (Figure 11c).[135] In another work, Ni-containing POM, $\mathrm{\small{[Ni_{4}(H_{2}O)_{2}(P W_{9}O_{34})_{2}]^{10-}}~(N i_{4}P_{2}}$ , was encapsulated into the pores of phosphorescent MOFs built from $[\mathrm{Ir}(\mathsf{p p y})_{2}(\mathsf{b p y})]^{+}$ and $[\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}]^{2+}$ -derived dicarboxylate ligands using the same method.[129] The proximity of $\mathbf{Ni}_{4}\mathbf{P}_{2}$ to multiple photosensitizers in $\mathbf{Ni}_{4}\mathbf{P}_{2}@\mathrm{MOF}$ allows the facile multielectron transfer to $\mathbf{Ni}_{4}\mathbf{P}_{2}$ sites for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution with a TON as high as 1476. The $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution reaction proceeds by the oxidative quenching of the excited photosensitizer by $\mathbf{Ni}_{4}\mathbf{P}_{2}$ as the initiating step based on photophysical and electrochemical studies. Further, a palladium complex $\mathrm{Pd(H_{2}b p y d c)C l_{2}}$ $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bpydc}=2,2^{\prime}$ -bipyridine- $^{5,5^{\prime}}$ - dicarboxylic acid) was directly incorporated into UiO-67(Zr).[126] The use of a large amount of the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ bpdc ligand in UiO-67(Zr) which does not chelate the Pd complex leads to the uniform distribution of isolated Pd single active sites in the MOF. The encapsulated Pd single active site efficiently activates $\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C l}$ bond in the Heck and Suzuki–Miyaura coupling reactions of aryl chlorides bearing a variety of substituents.
Despite many reviews that have summarized the incorporation of metal clusters into the pores of MOFs,[24,29,33,47] typical examples of the incorporation of metal clusters into the pore of COFs have not been described. A N-rich $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{P}$ -Bph-COF was employed as a support to incorporate Pd clusters with an average size of about $2\ \mathrm{nm}$ in a uniformly dispersed manner. The periodically distributed rich N atoms results in the stabilization of Pd ions inside the COF.[130] Such a $\mathrm{Pd}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{P}$ -Bph-COF composite displays excellent catalytic activity toward Suzukicoupling reaction between bromoarenes and arylboronic acids under mild condition. High yields of $97.1\mathrm{-}98.5\%$ were obtained. Further, the stability and reusability of the catalyst were proved by simple separation and recycle with activity and selectivity retained for several times. Covalent triazine frameworks (CTFs) are a class of microporous conjugated polymers linked together with 1,3,5-triazine units. They potentially serve as novel platforms for supported catalysts owing to the abundant nitrogen atoms with lone pairs for metal coordination. The isolated Pt single atoms of less than $0.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ can be uniformly dispersed on a hybrid CTF/C material due to the formation of $\mathrm{Pt-N}$ coordination evidenced by EXAFS.[62] Pt-CTF/C exhibits efficient electrocatalytic activity for oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) in acidic solution with a high methanol tolerance, which is a very attractive property for direct methanol fuel cells as the methanol-crossover effect is one of the issues to be addressed. As shown in Figure 11d, the same catalyst also exhibits superior electrocatalytic hydrogen oxidation reaction (HOR) activity without the requirement for the overpotential, compared to commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ because of the abundant atomically dispersed Pt.[137] $\mathrm{Co_{\scriptscriptstyleX}N i_{\gamma}(O H)_{2}\mathrm{[139]}}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{N}^{[140]}$ were confined into the pores of COF for superior oxygen evolution reaction (OER) activity in the alkaline solution. The periodic structure of the IISERP-COF3 makes it an ideal matrix to disperse the catalytically active $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}\mathrm{N}$ nanoparticles favoring their high accessibility and thereby good charge transport within the composite, requiring only a low overpotential of $230~\mathrm{mV}$ at $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ .[140]
## 3.5. Metal Node-Based Active Sites in MOFs
Usually, the metal ions in MOFs can either directly serve as active sites (Section 3.5.1) or provide opportunity to form the catalytic sites by cation doping in metal nodes (Section 3.5.2) and attaching single sites on metal nodes (Section 3.5.3), thus precisely affording well-defined catalysts as molecular active sites for catalytic reactions.
## 3.5.1. Metal Ions or Metal Clusters as Nodes
Open metal ions or metal clusters in geometric frameworks of MOFs can be accessible to reactant substrates through the channels of the frameworks and serve as molecular active sites for catalytic reactions after the activation of MOFs by removing labile solvent molecules or counter ions. Some representative works are listed in Table 6.[143–169] For example, a Pd-MOF with the molecular formula of $[\mathrm{Pd}(2\cdot\mathrm{pymo})_{2}]_{n}$ (2-pymo $=2$ -hydroxypyrimidinolate) was fabricated to form the 3D sodalite-type framework possessing two different hexagonal windows with free openings of 4.8 and $8.8\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , characterized by X-ray diffraction and DRUV–vis spectroscopy.[165] Owing to substrate shape selectivity, the Pd-MOF was found active for hydrogenation of 1-octene and showed no activity for hydrogenation of cyclododecene. The conventional $\mathrm{Pd/C}$ has no such selectivity. Similarly, high efficiency and size-dependent selectivity on catalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ cycloaddition with small epoxides, such as 2-methyloxirane $96\%$ yield) and 2-ethyloxirane $83\%$ yield), were achieved over $\{\mathrm{Cu}_{4}[(\mathrm{C}_{57}\mathrm{H}_{32}\mathrm{N}_{12})(\mathrm{COO})_{8}]\}_{n}$ MOF with the exposed Lewis-acid metal sites, inherent high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity, and the confinement effect of the pore size.[156] When the large substrates such as 1,2-epoxyoctane, 1,2-epoxydodecane, and 2-ethylhexyl glycidyl were employed in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ cycloaddition reaction, the product yields sharp decreased to less than $10\%$ under the reaction conditions, indicating the high selectivity toward the size of the substrates. A $Z\mathrm{n}$ -polyhedral MOF, $[\mathrm{Zn}_{6}(\mathrm{TATAB})_{4}$ $(\mathrm{DABCO})_{3}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{3}]\cdot12\mathrm{DMF}\cdot9\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ with abundant amine sites in the linkers exhibits an excellent catalytic efficiency in the cycloaddition of small propylene oxide with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ into propylene carbonate under mild conditions of $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and ambient $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ pressure. The extraordinary high yield is attributed to activation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ at the basic amine sites to form a carbamate species which then reacts with epoxides adsorbed on the adjacent $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ Lewis acidic sites.[150]
MOF photocatalysts with the organic linkers as the photosensitizer and metal nodes as the active site have been widely reported. For example, ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2^{-}}{\mathrm{MIL}}{\cdot}125(\mathrm{Ti})$ was reported as visible light-responsive photocatalysts for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction to ${\mathrm{HCOO^{-}}}$ during which the electron transfers from the photoexcited organic ligand to reduce Ti(IV) sites to the active Ti(III) sites for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction.[149] The construction of ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2^{-}}{\mathrm{MIL}}{\cdot}125(\mathrm{Ti})$ not only incorporates high density of the immobilized Ti(IV) sites, but also allows full access of these active sites via the welldefined pore channel. The introduction of $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ groups into the linkers allows the extension of absorption to the visible light region and an increase of adsorption capability of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . In a more recent work, the photocatalytic activity of MIL-125(Ti) for oxidation of benzyl alcohol to benzaldehyde was found varying with the degree of amination of the linker. A linear increase of the activity with the percentage of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ linker was established below $50\%$ amination followed by a plateau.[143] Although the use of aminated linkers is associated with improved optical absorption, the complete amination of the organic linker does not lead to the optimized photocatalytic activity, owing to the limitation of the participation of additional aminated linkers for generating $\mathrm{Ti^{3+}{-}O{-}T i^{4+}}$ pairs as catalytic centers. In another study, a very stable mesoporous zirconium−porphyrin MOF (PCN-222) with $\ensuremath{\mathrm{zr}}_{6}$ centers as the catalytic sites was found able to selectively photoreduce $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to $\mathsf{H C O O^{-}}$ with high efficiency under visible-light irradiation owing to efficient visible light absorption by ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{TCPP}}$ ligand, the high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity, and the effective inhibition of the detrimental radiative electron−hole recombination.[166] Interestingly, the 2D netted architecture of $\mathrm{[Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{PymS)}_{4}]_{n}$ $\mathrm{(PymSH=}$ pyrimidine-2-thiol) MOF is not only highly stable and insoluble in water, but also allows the exposure of active metal nodes to the reactant substrates. Thus, it might serve as a potential catalyst for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production under aqueous conditions.[148]
It is relatively hard to control the characteristics of unsaturated metal sites after the MOFs being synthesized. Rational design of the metal nodes at the synthesis stage is more desirable. In a recent work, a laser-chemical method was developed to induce the fabrication of Ni(TPA/TEG) MOF with a considerably high defect concentration by cross-linking together TPA as a rigid linker and TEG as a soft linker (Figure 12), characterized by STEM images, energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy, and XRD patterns.[161] The open and defective structure of $\mathrm{Ni(TPA/}$ TEG) MOF allows $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ active sites to be more accessible for capture and stabilization of the $\mathbf{CO2^{\cdot-}}$ intermediates, leading to high activity for reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to CO. Remarkably, a near $100\%$ CO selectivity over other gases was achieved (Figure 12b). The zero/ultralow selectivity to $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ by proton reduction was attributed to unfavorable binding of H· on the Ni-TPA active sites. When the spongy Ni(TPA/TEG) MOF is further decorated with $\mathbb{R}\mathrm{h}$ or $\mathrm{Ag}$ cocatalysts, CO can be further reduced to other products such as formic acid and acetic acid through protoncoupled multielectron reaction processes (Figure 12c–e).
In an earlier study, Cu-bipy-BTC MOF was found to exhibit excellent electrocatalytic activity toward ORR via 4-electron reduction of $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ owing to the redox process of $\mathrm{{Cu}^{\mathrm{{II/I}}}}$ open metal sites.[159] However, the poor chemical stability and the poor electronic conductivity limit its catalytic performance. Cu-tedbdc-MOF supported on graphene oxide (GO)[152] and Fe-TCPP MOF on pyridine-functionalized graphene[151] were explored for ORR, during which GO or graphene acts a good electron transfer mediator to afford facile 4-electron ORR pathway. The
2D $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ $(\mathrm{HITP}=2,3,6,7,10,11$ -hexaiminotriphenylene) MOF also shows high ORR catalytic activity comparable with the most active Pt-free electrocatalysts owing to the highly active $\mathrm{Ni-N_{4}}$ sites embedded in MOF, the high surface area and large porosity for $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ accessibility, and the high conductivity $(40\thinspace\mathrm{S~cm^{-1}})$ .[160] MOFs also have emerged as catalysts for OER and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ electrocatalytic reduction. A 3D noninterpenetrating MOF, $[\mathrm{Pb}_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{TCPP})]\cdot4\mathrm{DMF}\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (Pb-TCPP), was shown as a low-cost and earth-abundant metallic electrocatalyst for OER but with low activity.[146] The 2D CoBDC (cobalt 1,4-benzenedicarboxylate) MOF in situ hybridized on the surfaces of $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\boldsymbol{x}}$ (the MXene phase) nanosheets was used as the catalyst for OER.
The current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ at a potential of $1.64\mathrm{~V~}$ (vs RHE) and a Tafel slope of $48.2~\mathrm{mV}$ decade−1 was achieved in $0.1~\mathrm{~\r~{~M~}~}$ KOH, as the electrically conductive and hydrophilic $\mathrm{Ti}_{3}\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{T}_{\boldsymbol{x}}$ nanosheets enable the rapid charge and ion transfer across the well-defined hybrid interface and facilitate the access of aqueous electrolyte to the catalytically active CoBDC surfaces.[169] Interestingly by a simple exchange process, $\scriptstyle{\big[}\mathbf{Co}_{2}(\mu\mathbf{-}$ $\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\mathrm{btta})]$ (MAF-X27-OH) can be easily obtained from ${\lbrack\mathsf{C o}_{2}(\mu}\cdot$ $\mathrm{Cl})_{2}$ (btta)] (MAF-X27-Cl, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bbta}=1H,5H$ -benzo $(1,2-d;4,5-d^{\prime})$ bistriazole) with open metal sites. The incorporated $\scriptstyle\mu\mathrm{-OH}$ ligands and retained open metal sites give rise to greatly improved electrocatalytic OER activities with an overpotential of $292~\mathrm{mV}$ at $10.0\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ in $1.0\mathrm{~M~}$ KOH solution (Figure 13).[158] Isotope labeling experiments further confirmed that the hydroxide ligands are involved in a low-energy intraframework coupling pathway during the OER process (Figure $^{13\mathrm{b-g})}$ . Sheet-like $z_{\mathrm{n-}}$ BTC MOF deposited on carbon paper was used as the cathode for reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ with imidazolium based ionic liquids as the electrolytes. An efficient reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ with about $88\%$ selectivity was achieved. The fluorine species in imidazolium based ionic liquids strongly interacts with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and the coordinated $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ of Zn-MOF adsorbs CO intermediate product to be further reduced into $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ .[155]
## 3.5.2. Cation Doping in Metal Nodes
Cation doping in metal nodes, i.e., the substitutes of metal nodes by other metal cations in MOFs, is another way to create catalytically active sites.[170] As shown in Figure 14, postexchange (Figure 14a) or one-step synthesis methods (Figure 14b) can be generally utilized to substitute the metal nodes with other cations. Table 7 lists typical examples about cation doping of MOFs for catalytic applications.[171–182]
Earlier, Dincˇa and co-workers reported the use of postexchange method to homogeneously substitute $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ in $\mathrm{{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{{O}}}$ cluster of MOF-5 by $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ , $\scriptstyle{\dot{\mathsf{V}}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{V}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Cr}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}$ , $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Mn}}^{2+}$ , and $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ (Figure 15a), which was characterized by powder X-ray diffraction patterns, diffuse reflectance UV–vis–NIR spectra and DRIFTS spectra. The crystal structure of the MOF does not change after substitution.[171] It was found that the foreign metal ions are coordinated within a unique all-oxygen trigonal ligand field and are thus accessible to both inner- and outersphere oxidants. $\mathrm{Cr}^{2+}{\cdot}\mathrm{MOF}{\cdot}5$ with $\mathsf{N O B F}_{4}$ in acetonitrile caused the rapid formation of blue $\mathrm{(BF4)Cr^{3+}}$ -MOF-5 owing to outersphere electron transfer. On the other hand, $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ -doped MOF-5 activates NO via electron transfer from the $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ center by inner sphere oxidation. Furthermore, it was shown that $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ cation in $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}.$ -doped MOF-5 disproportionates nitric oxide to produce nitrous oxide and a ferric nitrito complex (Figure 15b) owing to the formation of the $_{\mathrm{N-N}}$ bond at single-isolated metal sites from in situ diffuse reflectance FTIR spectroscopy and EXAFS.[172] These works demonstrate that inserting reactive cations into SBUs of MOFs are convenient methods for the generation of versatile coordination sites potentially for unprecedented redox reactions.
Doping Ti(IV) into $\mathrm{UiO-}66(\mathrm{Zr}){\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ MOF via postsynthetic exchange has led to an efficient photocatalyst for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction to HCOOH under visible light irradiation. Transient absorption spectroscopy was used to study a series of mixed-metal UiO-6 $6(\mathrm{Zr/Ti})–\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ with different percentages of exchanged $\mathrm{Ti^{4+}}$ ions.[174,176] The spectroscopic evidences revealed that the substituted $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}$ acts as a mediator to facilitate electron transfer from the excited ligand to the $(\mathrm{Zr/Ti})_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}$ nodes in mixed $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr}/\mathrm{Ti}){-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ where photocatalytic reduction should occur. A well-defined and single-isolated $\mathrm{{Ni}(I I)}$ active site in Ni-doped MFU-4l is also created for selective dimerization of ethylene to 1-butene.[175] This catalyst offers a turnover frequency of $11.5\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ with 1-butene selectivity up to $96.2\%$ , exceeding the selectivity reported for the current industrial dimerization process.
Postsynthetic metal exchange can also be used to realize single-crystal to single-crystal transformation via the complete substitution of original metal moieties in MOFs. For example, $\mathrm{[Cd_{6}(B P T)_{4}C l_{4}(H_{2}O)_{4}]\cdot[C_{44}}$ $\mathrm{H}_{36}\mathrm{N}_{8}\mathrm{CdCl}]\cdot[\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{O}]$ ·[solvent] (porp $\boldsymbol{\mathbf{\ell}}_{1}@\boldsymbol{\mathrm{MOM}}$ - 10) represents an ideal candidate for complete postsynthetic metal exchange because their weak field ligands dissociate readily.[181] Mnporph $\mathscr{@\mathrm{MOM-10-Mn}}$ in the formula of $\mathrm{\Delta[Mn(II)_{6}(B P T)_{4}C l_{4}(C H_{3}O H)_{4}]\cdot[C_{44}H_{36}N_{8}M n}$ (III)]·Cl·[solvent] was prepared by completely substituting $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ with $\mathrm{{Mn}(I I)}$ . The resultant Mnporph $@$ MOM-10-Mn with $\mathrm{{Mn}(I I)}$ as the catalytic sites was used for epoxidation of trans-stilbene with $75\%$ conversion, while porph $\varpi\mathrm{MOM}{\cdot}10$ only yielded $7\%$ conversion. In addition, some metal moieties in the MOFs can be selectively exchanged to catalytic metal moieties for catalysis. For example, the $\mathrm{Fe(na)}_{4}(\mathrm{L}^{\mathrm{T}})$ fragment in $[\{\mathrm{Fe}_{3}(\mu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{O})(\mathrm{bdc})_{3}\}_{4}\{\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{na})_{4}(\mathrm{L}^{\mathrm{T}})\}_{3}]$ $\begin{array}{r l}{(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bdc}}&{{}=}\end{array}$ 1,4-benzenedicaboxylic acid, ${\mathrm{Hna}}=$ nicotinic acid) MOF can be removed to give $\smash{[\{\mathrm{Fe}_{3}(\mu_{3}-\mathrm{O})$ $(\mathrm{bdc})_{3}\}_{4}]$ with a unique framework connectivity possessing suitable distribution of open metal sites for binding the dicobalt cluster in the form of $\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{na})_{4}(\mathrm{L}^{\mathrm{T}})_{2}$ .[177] After the singlecrystal to single-crystal transformation process, a thermal-, water- and alkaline-stable MOF $[\{\mathrm{Fe}_{3}(\mu_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{O})(\mathrm{bdc})_{3}\}_{4}\{\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{na})_{4}(\mathrm{L}^{\mathrm{T}})_{2}\}_{3}]$ containing the desired dicobalt clusters was obtained, giving high electrocatalytic activity for OER in water at a $\mathrm{\pH}$ of 13 with overpotential as low as $225~\mathrm{mV}$ at $10\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ .
Besides doping, preparation of bimetallic MOFs via one-step synthesis has also been frequently used to prepare high-performance catalysts (Figure 14b). The incorporation of two different metal nodes in the same framework may generate defects in MOFs. The synergistic effects between different metals may enhance their intrinsic properties.[183] Fe/Co bimetallic MOFs prepared by onestep hydrothermal method exhibit excellent bifunctional catalytic activities for OER and ORR. The presence of Fe and Co coordination centers in MOF provides a different type of catalytic environment for oxygen diffusion and catalytic site utilization.[179] Further, Fe/ Ni-BTC MOF (btc $^{\circ}={1,3,5}$ -benzenetricarboxylic acid) thin films loaded on nickel foam by electrochemical deposition show outstanding electrocatalytic activity for OER with a low overpotential of $270~\mathrm{mV}$ at $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , a small Tafel slope of $47\mathrm{mV}$ decade−1 and great stability, owing to the open channels for electrolyte or $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ diffusion, copious exposed active centers homogeneously distributed in MOF, and the Fe/Ni synergistic effect.[180] The bimetal–organic framework nanosheets (NiCo-UMOFNs) synthesized from a mixed solution of $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ , ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ and benzenedicarboxylic acid also exhibited high electrocatalytic activity for OER in alkane solutions $(1~\mathrm{~M~}~\mathrm{KOH})$ .[182] NiCo-UMOFN on glassy-carbon electrodes required an overpotential of $250~\mathrm{mV}$ to achieve a current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ . When the MOF nanosheets are loaded on copper foam, this decreases to $189~\mathrm{mV}$ with a low onset potential of $1.39{\mathrm{~V~}}$ (vs RHE). Moreover, the NiCo-UMOFNs exhibited an impressive long-term stability $(200\mathrm{h})$ at a constant overpotential of $0.25~\mathrm{V},$ with the associated Faradaic efficiency as high as $99.3\%$ . A combination of X-ray absorption spectroscopy analysis and densityfunctional theory (DFT) calculations revealed that the coordinatively unsaturated metal atoms within the porous ultrathin NiCo-UMOFN layers are the dominating active centers and the coupling effect between Ni and Co metals is crucial for tuning the electrocatalytic activity.
## 3.5.3. Single Sites Attached to Metal Nodes
The attachment of catalytic sites on the metal nodes of MOFs provides an opportunity to precisely afford well-defined catalysts as molecular active sites for catalytic reactions. The structurally well-defined $-\mathrm{OH}/{-\mathrm{OH}_{2}}$ groups on metal nodes of MOFs such as ${\mathrm{UiO}}{\cdot}66({\mathrm{Zr}})$ and NU- $\cdot1000(\mathrm{Zr})$ could behave as isolated sites to bind the metal-containing species via vapor-phase atomic layer deposition (ALD) in MOFs (AIM) and solution-phase deposition in MOFs (SIM). In this way, single catalytic sites attached to the metal nodes can be created for heterogeneous catalysis. Some representative works are summarized in Table 8.[184–206]
SIM is a popular method to graft the metal species on the nodes,[184–189,193–195,197–199,203–206] since it does not involve specialized equipment and occurs at ambient temperature and pressure. Four criteria in SIM metalation of MOFs should to be met,[206] including (1) the terminal ${-\mathrm{OH}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ groups poised for postsynthetic metalation, (2) the channels decorated with reactive terminal ${-\mathrm{OH}/\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ ligands for diffusion and metalation reaction of metal ions, (3) a certain degree of framework flexibility to adapt to structural changes during the metalation, and (4) high stability to maintain the crystallinity. PCN$700(\mathrm{Zr})^{[206]}$ with 8-connected $\left[\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{8}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}\right]$ clusters is such a platform for postsynthetic cluster metalation via SIM owing to the low connectivity, high accessibility, flexibility, and good stability. In this MOF, $\mathrm{M}^{2+}$ ( $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{Ni}$ , Co) reacts with the $\mu_{3}{\mathrm{-OH}}$ and terminal $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ ligands on the $\left[\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{8}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}\right]$ cluster to afford a new $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{M}_{4}$ cluster (Figure 16). Single-crystal X-ray diffraction analysis provided snapshots of the structural transformation process of cooperative cluster metalation and ligand migration processes. $\mathrm{In}^{3+}$ was also metalated in PCN$700(\mathrm{Zr})$ via SIM method, which showed excellent catalytic activity in the acetaldehyde cyclotrimerization reaction. Similarly, $\operatorname{Ir}(\mathbf{CO})_{2}(\mathsf{a c a c})$ (acac $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ acetylacetonate) and $\mathrm{Ir}(\mathsf C_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4})_{2}(\mathsf{a c a c})$ as precursors react with functional groups such as $-\mathrm{OH}$ on the nodes of NU-1000(Zr) or UiO-66(Zr) to form single iridium site as characterized by IR and EXAFS.[205] $\mathrm{Ir}({\mathrm C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4})_{2}$ anchored NU$1000(\mathrm{Zr})$ or ${\mathrm{UiO}}{\cdot}66({\mathrm{Zr}})$ showed high activity for ethylene hydrogenation with a selectivity of $99.5\%$ and $98.5\%$ , respectively. Moreover, the Mo(VI) oxide catalyst attached to the nodes of $\mathrm{NU}{-}1000(\mathrm{Zr})$ gives rise to high conversion for cyclohexene epoxidation and exceptionally good stability without the leaching of the active species, which are crucial factors for a heterogeneous catalyst but are lacking for $\mathrm{Mo-}\mathrm{Zr}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (Figure 17).[198] The results from the DFT calculations revealed that the leaching of Mo species from $\mathsf{N U}.1000(\mathsf{Z r})$ is endergonic in nature which makes Mo-NU-1000(Zr) a stable catalyst for cyclohexene epoxidation.
It is notable that a simple and low-cost strategy of producing earth-abundant metal catalysts at the nodes of MOF was developed by Lin and co-workers.[187,188,195] The straightforward deprotonation of $-\mathrm{OH}$ sites in SBUs of MOFs with nBuLi followed by reaction with metal ions in THF affords efficient and recyclable single-site MOF catalysts for various types of organic reactions, such as chemoselective borylation, silylation, amination of benzylic $\mathrm{C-H}$ bonds and hydrogenation, and hydroboration of alkenes and ketones. For example, CoCl and FeBr single-site catalysts supported on ${\mathrm{UiO}}{\cdot}68({\mathrm{Zr}})$ were prepared by this method.[195] Kinetic studies and EXAFS characterization suggest that such organic transformation occurs on electrondeficient, coordinatively unsaturated, and isolated metal centers at the SBUs. The reaction proceeds via s-bond metathesis pathways due to the steric environment around the catalytic site. M-MTBC $\mathrm{{\Delta}M=Z r}$ or Hf, $\mathrm{MTBC=}$ methane-tetrakis $\lceil p$ -biphenylcarboxylate)) with the new $\mathrm{Zr_{8}(}\mu_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{O})_{8}(\mu_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{OH})_{4}$ nodes were used to anchor CoCl sites via the same method, followed by reducing them with $\mathrm{NaBEt}_{3}\mathrm{H}$ to form $\mathrm{CoH}$ catalysts. $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MTBC-CoH is highly tolerant to many functional groups and displays high activity in the hydrogenation of 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene with a TON greater than 8000.[188]
Compared to SIM, AIM is an effective strategy to produce single-site catalysts and can circumvent active site sintering. This method also eliminates purification and activation steps and therefore avoids problems related to site blocking by solvent molecules.[184,190–193,196,200–202] Three design factors are of exceptional importance to an MOF broadly suitable for AIM: (1) mesoporous (instead of microporous) channels to facilitate the diffusion of ALD reactants within the MOFs, (2) thermal stability between 100 and $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , and (3) spatially oriented functional groups (e.g. $-\mathrm{OH}$ groups) for metalation reactions. The NU-1000(Zr) MOF was designed as an excellent platform to quantitatively deposit $\mathsf{A l}_{x}\mathsf{O}_{\gamma}$ on $\mathrm{Zr(IV)}$ sites from trimethylaluminum precursors owing to its large 1D pores and apertures with two pore diameters at around 12 and $\bar{3}0\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , the high thermal stability up to $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and spatially isolated $-\mathrm{OH}$ groups on the $\ensuremath{\mathrm{zr}}_{6}$ -cluster nodes.[196] Lewis acidic Al(III) on $\mathrm{Zr(IV)}$ sites serves as site-isolating catalytic sites toward the effective Knoevenagel condensation, compared with the pristine $\mathrm{NU}{-}1000(\mathrm{Zr})$ that is inactive. Moreover, the sinteringresistant single-site Ni catalyst was uniformly and precisely installed on the nodes of NU-1000(Zr) by the AIM method, which exhibits excellent catalytic activity for gas-phase alkene hydrogenation/oligomerization with long-term stability.[192] As shown in Figure 18, $\mathrm{NiS}_{x}$ clusters deposited on $\mathrm{NU}{-}1000(\mathrm{Zr})$ via AIM method also serve as cocatalyst for photocatalytic hydrogen evolution. The pyrene-based linker in NU-1000(Zr)
acts as a UV sensitizer and transfers an electron to the adjacent $\mathrm{NiS}_{x}$ clusters.[201] Also note that the AIM method was utilized to deposit ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ ions throughout the entire MOF film consisting of the freestanding single-crystalline and sub-micrometer rods, which was anticipated to greatly boost the areal density or electrode-surface concentration of Co catalytic site for OER, relative to what can be achieved via conventional monolayer coating of the electrode with catalytic moieties.[190]
The AIM and SIM approaches were compared to deposit ${\mathrm{Co}}(\mathrm{II})$ ions on the nodes of $\mathrm{NU}{-}1000(\mathrm{Zr})$ MOF.[193] Co-SIM/ ${\tt V U-}1000(\mathrm{Zr})$ and $\mathrm{Co{-AIM/NU{-}1000}{(Z r)}}$ are found to be active for catalytic oxidative dehydrogenation of propane to propene at reaction temperatures as low as around $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Interestingly, different catalytic activity and selectivity toward propene formation were observed under the same experimental conditions for these two catalysts. The behavioral differences are consistent with the presence of different Co active sites evidenced by in situ EXAFS results. In particular, upon activation in $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , a new ${\mathrm{Co}}{\cdots}{\mathrm{Co}}$ interaction appears for Co-AIM/NU-1000(Zr), which is absent in Co-SIM/NU-1000(Zr).
## 4. Theoretical Studies
Although substantial works have been carried out for the rational design of MOF and COF-based molecular solid catalysts, theoretical and kinetic studies revealing the reaction mechanism in these frameworks are scarce, especially the kinetic studies. Several reports can be found using DFT calculations to predict and clarify the reaction mechanisms based on adsorption/desorption energy, the activation energy, and the barriers of reaction pathway. Compared with the conventional solid catalysts with less defined surface properties, the structurally homogeneous MOF and COF catalysts allow the extension of the calculated results based on the representative clusters to the entire body of the catalysts.
The adsorption of reactant substrates plays an important role during heterogeneous catalysis. Recently, by cutting out from the crystallographically determined structure of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ , a finite size cluster model was used to investigate the molecular adsorption behavior of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , CO, and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ on $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ sites using DFT calculation.[207] The results show that $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , and CO strongly adsorb on the reduced $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ site compared to the $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ site. The electron transfer from $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ to $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and CO causes the chemisorptive interaction of these gas molecules with the $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ ion, which is not observed over $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . In another work, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption on MOF-253 salt-chelated with $\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}$ or $\operatorname{Cu}(\mathtt{B F}_{4})_{2}$ was investigated.[208] Following salt chelation on the bipyridine ligand of the parent MOF-253, the two $\mathrm{~N~}$ atoms preferentially orient to one side to bind the salt. During $\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}$ insertion, dipole polarization induces multiple adsorption sites, including PdCl bonds and colinker sites, although $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption energy is not obviously increased $(-0.21$ to $-0.24~\mathrm{eV})$ . In contrast, the $\operatorname{Cu}(\mathtt{B F}_{4})_{2}$ chelation significantly increases the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption energy to $-0.49\mathrm{eV},$ because the large positive charge of the $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ ion induces more significant electrostatic interaction with the O atom of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . The $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ binding energy also obviously increases by $7~\mathrm{kJ~mol^{-1}}$ after $\operatorname{Cu}(\mathrm{BF}_{4})_{2}$ being loaded on MOF-253. This work provides some insights into the selection of a salt for chelation with the open bipyridine linker to improve gas uptake and achieve the potential catalytic applications.
Similar to other solid catalysts, lower activation energy barriers of reaction pathway can affect the efficiency and selectivity of MOF and COF catalysts. For example, the reaction mechanism of the hydrogenation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to HCOOH over Cu-alkoxide-functionalized MOF-5 was investigated by DFT calculation with the $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{M}06{\cdot}\mathrm{L}$ functional. It was shown that the reaction can proceed via two different pathways, namely, concerted and stepwise mechanisms.[209] In the concerted mechanism, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is one-step hydrogenated to generate HCOOH with a large activation barrier of $67.2\mathrm{kcalmol^{-1}}$ . In the stepwise mechanism, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is hydrogenated to a formate intermediate with an activation energy of $24.2\ \mathrm{kcal\mol^{-1}}$ , and formate is further hydrogenated to HCOOH with an activation energy of $18.3\mathrm{\kcal\mol^{-1}}$ . Due to the smaller activation barriers associated with stepwise pathway, it therefore seems to be more favored than the concerted pathway. A similar calculation was performed on UiO-67 functionalized with catalytic Lewis pair $(\mathrm{NBF}_{2})_{4}$ groups for hydrogenation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to methanol.[210] Three different reaction pathways were identified to produce methanol from $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and each pathway involves a series of concerted two-hydrogen transfer steps. It was found that reaction pathway of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\to{\mathit{c i s}}\mathrm{.HCOOH}\to$ $\mathrm{CH}_{2}(O H)_{2}\rightarrow\mathrm{CH}_{2}O\rightarrow\mathrm{CH}_{3}O H$ has the lowest potential energy surface owing to the effective heterolytic dissociation of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ to generate hydridic and protic $\mathrm{~H~}$ atoms bound to Lewis acid and base sites. The DFT calculations also showed that the undercoordinated sites from Cu-MOF may act as metal catalytic sites to promote the decomposition of hydroperoxide molecules such as ethylbenzene hydroperoxide.[211]
First-row transition metals (Fe, Co, Ni, Cu, and $Z\mathrm{n}$ ) supported on $\ensuremath{\mathrm{zr}}_{6}$ or $\mathrm{Hf}_{6}$ nodes of $\mathrm{NU}{-}1000(\mathrm{Zr})$ or NU-1000(Hf) have been computationally assessed for their activity for acceptorless alcohol dehydrogenation.[212] The proposed mechanism comprises the steps of (i) proton transfer, (ii) $\beta$ -hydride elimination, and (iii) $_\mathrm{H-H}$ bond formation, where the $\ensuremath{\mathrm{zr}}_{6}$ or $\mathrm{Hf}_{6}$ node support plays an active role in (i) and (iii). Among the metals evaluated, the Co and Ni derivatives are predicted to be more active catalysts with turnover-limiting free energies of activation of 28.5 and $26.5\mathrm{kcalmol^{-1}}$ , respectively. A combination of computational and experimental results was applied to compare Co and Ni supported on $\ensuremath{\mathrm{zr}}_{6}$ nodes of NU- $\cdot1000(\mathrm{Zr})$ via the AIM method for ethylene dimerization.[213] Three possible mechanistic pathways were proposed, among which the third pathway follows a Cossee–Arlman mechanism and it involves the preactivation of the M-AIM+NU-1000 acting as the catalyst. The insertion of ethylene into the ethylmetal bond was found as the rate-determining step in ethylene dimerization, with calculated activation energies of 24.1 and $15.6\ \mathrm{kcal\mol^{-1}}$ for mononuclear $\mathrm{Co-AIM+NU-1000}$ and Ni-AIM $\cdot+$ NU-1000, respectively. The greater activity over Ni(II) active site is attributed to transition-state stabilization associated with an empty 3d orbital that hybridizes more readily with relevant carbon p orbitals in low-spin Ni-AIM+NU-1000 than in high-spin Co$_{\mathrm{AIM+NU-1000}}$ . In addition, competing reaction of ethylene dimerization may happen at the ethylene hydrogenation reaction. The hydrogenation over $\mathrm{Ir}(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4})_{2}$ anchored $\ensuremath{\mathrm{zr}}_{6}$ node of NU-1000 is favored over dimerization because of the lower calculated barriers $(2{-}12\operatorname{kcal}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ for hydrogenation vs $8{-}19\mathrm{kcal}$ $\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ for dimerization).[205] Co deposited on the $\ensuremath{\mathrm{zr}}_{6}$ node of NU-1000 shows high selectivity for the oxidative dehydrogenation of propane to propene at reaction temperatures as low as around $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ owing to a low activation energy barrier for $\mathrm{C-H}$ bond abstraction.[193]
## 5. Challenges and Perspectives
Incorporation of reactive sites on solid supports has been a longstanding and well-utilized strategy in the fabrication of economically viable heterogeneous catalysts for many chemical conversion processes. Rationally designing MOF- and COFbased molecular solid catalysts is a promising avenue to achieve the ultimate goal of heterogeneous catalysis with high efficiency and desired selectivity, originating from their combined properties of high crystallinity, high surface area, flexible and tunable pore structure, and versatile chemical functionality at every part of the framework. Nonetheless, the majority of the research in this field is still at the early stage if aimed for industrial applications. While new discoveries are made rapidly, longstanding challenges have been presented among scientists and engineers with different expertise concerning their chemical and thermal stability, scalability, and production cost. Continued research efforts are needed to tackle these issues. In view of developing MOFs and COFs into practical solid catalysts with competitive advantages than the conventional solid catalysts, it is suggested that research activities might be focused on several aspects. First, more computational efforts can be spent to understand the mechanism of molecular interaction between the active site and the reactive species. Besides DFT calculations, other computational tools such as ab initio methods might also be used to reveal the chemical steps in the complex solid structures. Such information is believed to be critical for accelerating the searching progress of high-performance catalysts. Second, the clear evidences provided from the crystallographic, spectroscopic, and imaging methods allow the quantification of the active sites and sound interpretation of the catalytic mechanism. In combination with the computational study, these understandings will directly guide the design of MOF- and COF-based molecular solid catalysts. Further improvement in the characterization techniques, especially the use or development of in situ tools, is welcome. Lastly, the design of MOFs and COFs that can tolerate aqueous media particularly in acidic and basic environment and perhaps high temperature, and maintaining the long term stability of the frameworks for the industrial requirements, is of critical importance. Keeping the complexes intact in the framework while harnessing their catalytic activity under reaction condition is challenging but for certain worth further study.
Although great progresses have been achieved in rational design of molecular catalytic centers in crystalline frameworks, there are still formidable challenges for fabrication of economically viable and high-performance heterogeneous catalysts based on these systems. It is sincerely hoped that close collaborations among experimental and computational scientists with different and complementary expertise, as well as industrial partners, can rapidly evolve this field and eventually push successful candidates of molecular solid catalysts for industrial applications in the future.
